{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_is5hbjktyqw3wglprdi2q2fqsmie3lfbipkerziyj65fbxvdxcja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01UKchETTuuMqzXECbY47gbA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Build ERRORS.md error surface","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Qil4GP\n\nThis is PCRE2 (8-bit mode) C source in `c_src/`, translated to Rust in `translation/`.\n\nYour job: produce the file `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Qil4GP/translation/ERRORS.md` — an ERROR-SURFACE TABLE derived MECHANICALLY from the C source. Do NOT write any Rust or tests. Do NOT modify c_src/.\n\nMethod:\n1. The PUBLIC API is the set of `pcre2_*_8` functions exported from the .so. Get the list with:\n   `nm -D --defined-only c_src/build/libpcre2.so | awk '$2==\"T\"{print $3}' | sort`\n   (the library is already built). Focus on the PUBLIC entry points (`pcre2_compile_8`, `pcre2_match_8`, `pcre2_dfa_match_8`, `pcre2_substitute_8`, `pcre2_substring_*`, `pcre2_serialize_*`, `pcre2_pattern_info_8`, `pcre2_config_8`, `pcre2_*_context_*`, `pcre2_set_*`, `pcre2_match_data_*`, `pcre2_get_*`, `pcre2_convert_*`, `pcre2_maketables*`, `pcre2_jit_*`, `pcre2_code_copy*`, `pcre2_get_error_message_8`, etc.) — the ways an EXTERNAL CALLER can trigger a rejection.\n2. Mechanically grep the C sources for every distinct rejection/error path reachable from those entry points. Look for:\n   - `return PCRE2_ERROR_*` statements (grep -n 'return PCRE2_ERROR' c_src/src/*.c)\n   - `*errorcode = ERR<n>` / `errorcode = ERR<n>` assignments in pcre2_compile.c and friends\n   - `return NULL` paths in public constructors\n   - `return -1`, `return 0` sentinels\n   - explicit null-pointer checks (`== NULL`)\n   - explicit range checks / `if (x > MAX...)` / `if (x < ...)` and min/max constants\n   - invalid-option checks (e.g. `if ((options & ~PUBLIC_COMPILE_OPTIONS) != 0)`)\n   - `switch` default branches returning an error (e.g. pcre2_config_8, pcre2_pattern_info_8, pcre2_set_newline)\n   - UTF validity errors (PCRE2_ERROR_UTF8_ERR1..21 in pcre2_valid_utf.c)\n   - `PCRE2_ERROR_BADDATA`, `BADMAGIC`, `BADMODE`, `BADOFFSET`, `BADOPTION`, `NOMEMORY`, `NULL`, `NOSUBSTRING`, `NOUNIQUESUBSTRING`, `UNSET`, `PARTIAL`, `NOMATCH`, `DEPTHLIMIT`, `MATCHLIMIT`, `HEAPLIMIT`, `BADUTFOFFSET`, `BADSERIALIZEDDATA`, `MIXEDTABLES`, `BADREPLACEMENT`, `BADREPESCAPE`, `REPMISSINGBRACE`, `BADSUBSTITUTION`, `BADSUBSPATTERN`, `TOOMANYREPLACE`, `BADLENGTH`, etc.\n3. Write ONE ROW PER DISTINCT REJECTION, in a GitHub-markdown table with EXACTLY these columns:\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n\n- `function` = the PUBLIC entry point a test would call (name the internal helper in parentheses if useful).\n- `trigger` = the concrete invalid input/condition a differential test can CONSTRUCT from outside (e.g. \"pattern `a{2,1}` — quantifier max < min\", \"`pcre2_config_8` with unknown `what`=0x7fffffff\", \"`startoffset` > subject length\", \"match_data with ovecsize 0\", \"serialized bytes with corrupted magic\").\n- `expected C result` = the exact numeric/symbolic return value or sentinel (e.g. `NULL` + `*errorcode = ERR9 (=9)`, `PCRE2_ERROR_BADOPTION (-34)`, `PCRE2_ERROR_NOMATCH (-1)`).\n- Prefer triggers that are EXTERNALLY REACHABLE. If an error is only reachable internally, still include the row but note \"(internal)\" in the trigger.\n- Include the compile-time `ERR1..ERR100+` errors that are reachable via a concrete BAD PATTERN STRING. For those, give the actual pattern text. Look at the error message table in `c_src/src/pcre2_error.c` to map ERR numbers to meanings, and grep pcre2_compile*.c / pcre2_compile_class.c for the `ERR<n>` sites to find what input triggers each.\n- Also add a final section of generic-boundary rows every C API has: NULL pointers to each public function, zero/oversized lengths, out-of-range ENUM values crossing the FFI boundary (C enums accept any int).\n\nAim for THOROUGH coverage: this table should have at least 120 rows (PCRE2 has ~100 compile errors plus ~40 runtime errors). Group rows under `##` headings per source area (compile, match, dfa_match, substitute, substring, serialize, context/set_*, config, pattern_info, convert, match_data, valid_utf) and number rows continuously from 1.\n\nAdd a leading `[ ]` checkbox column is NOT needed for ERRORS.md — just the 4 columns above.\n\nVerify before finishing: the file exists, is valid markdown, has >=120 numbered rows (`grep -c '^| [0-9]' translation/ERRORS.md`). Report the final row count."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2078,"cache_read_input_tokens":29950,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2078,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f125a214-542c-4276-b4c6-bfe5147344e3","uuid":"98863dff-0b04-41f4-9fb9-c1f3ceef960b","timestamp":"2026-09-04T04:35:01.446Z"}
