wn_tables()`, ...),\n`compile_in(api, pattern, patlen, &cfg) -> Compiled` (field `.code`, auto-frees on\ndrop), `compile_both(pattern, patlen, &cfg, label) -> (Compiled, Compiled)`, and\n`PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_*` / `PCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED` / `PCRE2_UNSET` / `ERR_*` constants.\n\nStart your file with:\n```rust\nmod common;\nuse common::diff::*;\nuse common::*;\n```\nLook at `translation/tests/compile_match.rs` for the established style.\n\n## The C signature\n```c\nint pcre2_substitute(const pcre2_code *code, PCRE2_SPTR subject, PCRE2_SIZE length,\n  PCRE2_SIZE startoffset, uint32_t options, pcre2_match_data *match_data,\n  pcre2_match_context *mcontext, PCRE2_SPTR replacement, PCRE2_SIZE rlength,\n  PCRE2_UCHAR *outputbuffer, PCRE2_SIZE *outlengthptr);\n```\nIn the harness this is `Api.substitute` with Rust type\n`unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*const c_void, *const u8, usize, usize, u32, *mut c_void,\n*mut c_void, *const u8, usize, *mut u8, *mut usize) -> i32`.\n\n## What to cover (READ `c_src/src/pcre2_substitute.c` — 1795 lines — to derive these)\nWrite a central helper that, for a given (pattern, compile-opts, subject, replacement,\nsubstitute-opts, buffer size, startoffset), calls BOTH libraries and asserts EQUALITY of:\n  - the returned `int` rc,\n  - `*outlengthptr` on BOTH success AND failure (PCRE2 writes the needed length on\n    PCRE2_ERROR_NOMEMORY when SUBSTITUTE_OVERFLOW_LENGTH is set),\n  - the bytes actually written to the output buffer (only up to the length the C\n    library reports as written — do NOT compare uninitialised tail bytes; initialise\n    both buffers to the same sentinel like 0xAA and compare the whole buffer only when\n    rc >= 0, otherwise compare just the first `*outlengthptr` bytes if rc == 0/positive).\n\nThen drive it over these axes, using MANY randomized inputs per axis (fixed seed):\n1. Every `PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_*` flag alone and in combination:\n   GLOBAL, EXTENDED, UNSET_EMPTY, UNKNOWN_UNSET, OVERFLOW_LENGTH, LITERAL, MATCHED,\n   REPLACEMENT_ONLY — plus their pairwise/triple combos with GLOBAL.\n2. Replacement-string syntax (this is where the bugs live). Cover `$1`, `${1}`, `${name}`,\n   `$name`, `$$`, `$0`, `$&`-style forms the C actually supports, `\\$`, and every\n   EXTENDED escape the C handles: `\\U \\L \\u \\l \\E \\Q ... \\E`, `\\n \\r \\t \\f \\a \\e`,\n   `\\x41`, `\\x{1F600}`, `\\o{101}`, `\\0`, `${name:-default}` and `${name:+if:else}`\n   conditional forms, `$*MARK`, and nested/degenerate versions of each. Grep\n   pcre2_substitute.c for the exact set it accepts.\n3. Subject/replacement SHAPES: empty subject, empty replacement, replacement longer\n   than subject, matches at offset 0 / mid / at the very end, zero-length matches\n   (pattern `a*` / `(?=x)` / `\\b` with GLOBAL — the empty-match advance logic),\n   overlapping-looking matches, subject as PCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED, `startoffset` 0 /\n   mid / == length.\n4. Output-buffer sizing: buffer exactly the needed size, one byte too small, size 0,\n   size 1, and much larger — with and without SUBSTITUTE_OVERFLOW_LENGTH. Verify the\n   two-pass \"call with small buffer to learn the length, then call again\" workflow\n   agrees in both libraries.\n5. UTF: compile with PCRE2_UTF (and UTF|UCP), multi-byte subjects and replacements,\n   `\\U`/`\\L` case conversion over non-ASCII (this exercises the case tables), plus\n   invalid UTF-8 subjects with and without PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK.\n6. Named groups in the replacement, duplicate names (PCRE2_DUPNAMES), and unset\n   groups (interaction of UNSET_EMPTY vs UNKNOWN_UNSET vs neither).\n7. `PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_MATCHED` — pass a match_data that already holds a match from a\n   prior `pcre2_match` call, and one that holds NO match.\n8. A `pcre2_set_substitute_callout` callback: register a callback in BOTH libraries\n   that records (into a thread-local/static Vec) the fields of every\n   `pcre2_substitute_callout_block` it sees, and assert the two libraries produce the\n   SAME sequence of callout blocks. Read the struct layout from `c_src/include/pcre2.h`\n   and mirror it as a `#[repr(C)]` struct. Also test a callback that returns 0, >0\n   (skip this substitution) and <0 (abort) — the C treats these differently.\n9. `pcre2_set_substitute_case_callout` similarly (its callback does case conversion);\n   at minimum register one in both and compare results, including one that returns 0.\n10. Randomized fuzz: ~3000 iterations of random pattern + random subject + random\n    replacement (drawn from an alphabet heavy in `$ { } \\ 0-9 A-Z a-z : + - U L E Q`)\n    × a few option sets, asserting rc + outlength + bytes all agree.\n\n## Rules\n- Use `assert_eq!` with rich messages including the pattern/subject/replacement/options.\n- Skip a case when compilation fails in BOTH libraries (compare the errorcodes first via\n  `compile_both`, which already does that for you).\n- Guard against uninitialised-memory comparisons (see above) — that is the #1 source of\n  false failures. Also note `pcre2_get_ovector_pointer` entries beyond `2*rc` are\n  undefined.\n- Keep each `#[test]` fn under ~30 s. Use `RUST_MIN_STACK` -independent code (no deep\n  recursion).\n- The tests MUST pass. Iterate: `cd translation && cargo test --offline --test substitute -- --test-threads=1`\n  (build the release cdylib first with `cargo build --offline --release`).\n- If a test reveals a genuine C-vs-Rust divergence, FIX `translation/src/substitute.rs`\n  (or whichever module) to match the C exactly, rebuild, and re-run. Report every such\n  fix in your final message.\n\n## Deliverable / self-verification before you finish\n- `translation/tests/substitute.rs` exists, has >= 10 `#[test]` functions.\n- `cargo test --offline --test substitute -- --test-threads=1` reports 0 failed.\n- Report: number of tests, the exact final `test result:` line, and any Rust source\n  fixes you had to make (with the C line numbers that justified them).","uuid":"77fc77e1-9321-4ed3-af17-b49809f0db35","session_id":"f125a214-542c-4276-b4c6-bfe5147344e3"}
