ytes and every\n    length in the returned length vector; free with `substring_list_free`.\n  - `pcre2_next_match`: iterate it to exhaustion after a match and compare the full\n    sequence of (rc, offset-pair, mark) values.\nAlso compare `get_match_data_size`, `get_match_data_heapframes_size`,\n`get_ovector_count`, `get_startchar`, `get_mark` after each match.\nInclude a randomized section (fixed seed, >= 1500 iterations) with random subjects\nagainst the pattern corpus.\n\n## FILE 2 — tests/serialize.rs\nRead `c_src/src/pcre2_serialize.c` (295 lines).\nCover:\n  - `serialize_encode` of 1, 2, 3, ... N codes (including a large N like 30) compiled\n    from a varied pattern corpus; assert the produced byte blobs are IDENTICAL between\n    C and Rust, and that `*outlengthptr` agrees.\n  - `serialize_get_number_of_codes` on those blobs.\n  - `serialize_decode` round-trip: decode in the SAME library and assert every decoded\n    code behaves identically to the original (compare `pattern_info` for all selectors\n    and run matches); AND decode the C-produced blob in the C lib vs the Rust-produced\n    blob in the Rust lib and compare the resulting codes' `pattern_info` + match results.\n  - CROSS-DECODE: because the two libraries produce byte-identical blobs, feed the\n    C-produced blob to the RUST `serialize_decode` and vice versa, and assert the\n    decoded codes still match/behave identically. (Skip this if the blob embeds a\n    library-specific pointer — check `pcre2_serialize.c` first; PCRE2 stores only\n    offsets plus a magic/version header, so it should work.)\n  - Encoding with a custom general context (`general_context_create` with your own\n    malloc/free) so the memctl path is exercised in both.\n  - Codes compiled with the library's OWN `pcre2_maketables()` tables (this triggers the\n    PCRE2_ERROR_MIXEDTABLES path when combined with default-tables codes) — assert both\n    libraries return the same error.\n  - `serialize_free` on the blobs (no leak-crash).\n  - Decoding with `number_of_codes` smaller than, equal to, and larger than the blob's\n    actual count.\n\n## Rules\n- NEVER compare uninitialised memory. Only compare buffer bytes up to the length the C\n  library reports; only compare ovector pairs `0..rc`; initialise both buffers to the\n  same sentinel (0xAA) when you do compare whole buffers.\n- NEVER compare raw pointer VALUES between the two libraries — convert to offsets.\n- Free everything you allocate (`substring_free`, `substring_list_free`,\n  `serialize_free`, `match_data_free`, `code_free`, `*_context_free`,\n  `maketables_free`).\n- Use rich `assert_eq!` messages naming the pattern/subject/number/name/options.\n- Keep each `#[test]` under ~30 s.\n- Iterate until green:\n  `cd translation && cargo build --offline --release && cargo test --offline --test substring -- --test-threads=1`\n  and the same for `--test serialize`.\n- If a test reveals a genuine divergence, FIX the Rust source under `translation/src/`\n  to match the C exactly, rebuild, re-run. Report every fix.\n\n## Deliverable / self-verification before you finish\n- Both files exist; substring.rs has >= 8 `#[test]` fns, serialize.rs >= 6.\n- Both `cargo test --offline --test substring -- --test-threads=1` and\n  `--test serialize -- --test-threads=1` report `0 failed`.\n- Report the exact final `test result:` line for EACH file, and any Rust fixes made\n  (with the C line numbers justifying them).","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-ZdMRrI/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-Qil4GP/f125a214-542c-4276-b4c6-bfe5147344e3/tasks/a07033683633d02fa.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
