{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01FkYZmgQHYDjMDeNWWJUfNy","type":"tool_result","content":"1014\t/// Rows 296, 303, 317: documented UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR in the C library. These\n1015\t/// are NOT tested by calling them (the C library segfaults, so there is no\n1016\t/// observable \"correct\" result to compare against); they are recorded here so\n1017\t/// every ERRORS.md row is accounted for.\n1018\t///\n1019\t/// * row 296 — `pcre2_*_context_copy_8(NULL)` dereferences\n1020\t///   `ctx->memctl.malloc` with no NULL check (`pcre2_context.c:381`).\n1021\t/// * row 303 — `pcre2_config_8` with a `where` buffer smaller than the value.\n1022\t/// * row 317 — `pcre2_callout_enumerate_8` with `callback == NULL` on a pattern\n1023\t///   that contains a callout.\n1024\t///\n1025\t/// What we CAN verify is that the *documented* way of using each of them\n1026\t/// behaves identically, which the tests above already do.\n1027\t#[test]\n1028\tfn rows296_303_317_undefined_behaviour_documented() {\n1029\t    let (c, r) = both();\n1030\t    unsafe {\n1031\t        // row 317, safe half: callback == NULL on a pattern with NO callouts\n1032\t        // never invokes the callback, so it is well-defined.\n1033\t        for pat in [&b\"a\"[..], b\"abc\", b\"(a)(b)\"] {\n1034\t            let cc = compile_in(c, pat, pat.len(), &CompileCfg::new(0));\n1035\t            let rr = compile_in(r, pat, pat.len(), &CompileCfg::new(0));\n1036\t            assert_eq!(\n1037\t                (c.callout_enumerate)(cc.code, std::ptr::null_mut(), std::ptr::null_mut()),\n1038\t                (r.callout_enumerate)(rr.code, std::ptr::null_mut(), std::ptr::null_mut()),\n1039\t                \"callout_enumerate(no callouts, NULL cb) for {:?}\",\n1040\t                String::from_utf8_lossy(pat)\n1041\t            );\n1042\t        }\n1043\t    }\n1044\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01BsTNAemmrnQ1j1CTXzp7cG","session_id":"f125a214-542c-4276-b4c6-bfe514